course: dictionary
lesson: 10482
title: Turbulence: bumps in the air and trouble in the news
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Turbulence is a noun. Its main meaning is sudden, uneven movement of air or water, like the bumps you feel on a plane. «Turbulence is bumpy air, like waves in the sky.» The words bumpy and waves are your clues, so you can work out the meaning before you check.
explain: «We are flying through turbulence; please fasten your seat belts.» The words flying and seat belts tell you the plane is shaking. Swap in your meaning to check: "We are flying through sudden bumpy air" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Turbulence can also mean a time of confusion, trouble or sudden change. In the news, «political turbulence» means a country's government or leaders are having a rough and changing time. Context helps again: the word political tells you it is about how a country is run.
fact: Turbulence means sudden, uneven movement of air or water.
fact: «Turbulence» can make a plane shake or a boat rock.
fact: In science, smooth flow of air or water is different from turbulent flow, which is bumpy and uneven.
fact: «Turbulence» is uncountable, so you say "some turbulence", not "a turbulence".
fact: Turbulence can also mean a time of confusion, trouble or sudden change.
exercise: «Sudden bumpy air, called turbulence, can happen with no warning.» What does turbulence mean? | *Sudden, uneven movement of air that makes a plane shake | A kind of seat belt | A cloud shaped like a plane | A calm, smooth flight with no bumps
exercise: The pilot says «a little bit of bumpiness» means turbulence is coming. What should you do? | Stand up and walk to the toilet | Take off your shoes | Open the window to get some fresh air | *Stay belted in
exercise: Find and copy the word from «Pilots speak calmly so that nobody panics» that means feeling scared and out of control. | *panics | pilots | calmly | speak
test: The news says the country is in «political turbulence». What does turbulence mean here? | A hot, dry wind | A calm, peaceful summer | *A time of trouble and sudden change | A type of weather map
test: Why must you keep your seat belt fastened on a plane? | Because seat belts keep the food warm | *Because sudden bumps called turbulence can happen | Because the plane is going to land in the sea | Because the crew like to see everyone sitting still
exam: Which sentence uses turbulence to mean a time of trouble? | The plane shook in strong turbulence over the mountains | Turbulence can make a boat rock on the waves | *Political turbulence in the country made the news every day | The river had strong turbulence after the heavy rain
